    "reading": {
        "body": "Pennsylvania's tracked data center capacity totals just 11 MW across 14 facilities, with only two sites reporting any power figure at all, which means the true load of this inventory is almost certainly larger and largely unmeasured.\n\nThe two facilities with reported capacity, DataBank Pittsburgh PIT2 at 5 MW and Expedient Pittsburgh South at 6 MW, account for all 11 MW between them and together occupy roughly 73,000 square feet. Every other facility in the dataset carries no megawatt figure, including the Amazon AWS PHL site in Berwick and both Flexential locations. Absent power data for those facilities, the 11 MW total is a floor, not a ceiling.\n\nThe geographic spread matters for infrastructure reasoning. Pittsburgh and the Philadelphia suburbs, including King of Prussia, Collegeville, and Montgomery County, anchor most of the commercial colocation presence. The Karthaus facility, named explicitly as a power generator and compute center, and the Luzerne County Nautilus Cryptomine entries suggest that at least some capacity in this state is co-located with or defined by its power source rather than proximity to population centers, which is a pattern associated with power-constrained compute seeking cheap or dedicated generation. The dataset does not report grid interconnection details, construction status, or planned expansion for any facility, so nothing here speaks to future capacity or current constraint levels.",
